@meta
id: 20-16
title: Let vim do the arithmetic
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-expression
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: alt-tab to a calculator, alt-tab back, type the number
open: orders.csv

@brief
One register is not a place text is stored but a place text is
computed. `"=` is the expression register: type `"=`, and vim gives
you a prompt where you enter an expression; press Enter and the
result is what the register holds. `"=12*1849` Enter `p` puts `22188`.

The last row of this fixture file is missing its total. The order is
37 units at 1849 pence each. Do not reach for a calculator and do not
do it in your head: put the product at the end of the row.

@keys
| `"=` | the expression register — opens a prompt |
| `"=37*1849` `Enter` `p` | compute, then put the result |
| `Ctrl-R =` | the same prompt from inside insert mode (next lesson) |

@hints
Get to the end of the last line first, so the put lands after the
trailing comma.
--
`G$` and then `"=`. The prompt appears at the bottom of the screen.
Type the multiplication and press Enter; nothing is put until you press
`p`.
--
`G$` `"=37*1849` Enter `p`.

@solution
    G$ "=37*1849<Enter> p :wq<Enter>

The expression is vimscript, so it does more than sums: `"=line('.')`
puts the current line number, `"=strftime('%Y-%m-%d')` puts today's
date, `"=expand('%:t')` puts the file's name without its directory.
Anything vim can evaluate can be typed into a file this way.

One thing to know about the arithmetic: `/` on two whole numbers is
whole-number division, so `"=22188/100` gives `221`, not `221.88`. Write
`22188/100.0` when you want the decimals.

@start orders.csv
id,customer,status,total
ord_001,cus_042,PENDING,1999
ord_002,cus_017,SHIPPED,4500
ord_003,cus_042,CANCELLED,2750
ord_004,cus_003,SHIPPED,

@expected orders.csv
id,customer,status,total
ord_001,cus_042,PENDING,1999
ord_002,cus_017,SHIPPED,4500
ord_003,cus_042,CANCELLED,2750
ord_004,cus_003,SHIPPED,68413
